[QA 0] The paper determines the minimal embedding dimensions required for embedding periodic maps of surfaces into spheres, showing that for periodic maps of order \( n \geq 3g \) on a closed orientable surface of genus \( g \), the minimal dimension is 6 with specific exceptions for lower genera. | The key contributions of this paper can be summarized as:

The paper determines the minimal embedding dimension D_g(f) for embedding a periodic map f of order n ≥ 3g on a closed oriented surface of genus g into spheres, in the smooth and orientation-preserving category. Specifically, it shows that when n ≥ 3g and g > 0, D_g(f) = 6 with a few exceptions explicitly described. Furthermore, for any integer m > 1, it constructs infinitely many periodic maps f such that D_g(f) = m.
[QA 1] The paper establishes a Khintchine inequality with operator coefficients for arbitrary central Fourier series in a large class of non-Kac compact quantum groups, demonstrating a strong contrast to the lack of such inequalities in compact non-abelian Lie groups. | The key contribution of this paper is establishing a Khintchine inequality with operator coefficients for central Fourier series on a large class of non-Kac compact quantum groups, including the Drinfeld-Jimbo q-deformations G_q, the free orthogonal quantum groups O_F^+, and the quantum automorphism groups aut(B,ψ) with a δ-form ψ.
[QA 2] The paper demonstrates a transition from irregular to regular eutectic growth in the Al-Al₃Ni system by in situ observations during directional solidification, revealing the role of growth velocity and trijunction dynamics in governing this morphological transformation. | The key contribution of this paper is revealing the transition from irregular to regular eutectic growth in the Al-Al3Ni system through in-situ observations during directional solidification using optical microscopy and synchrotron transmission x-ray microscopy.
[QA 3] AnoPLe introduces a novel bi-directional prompt learning approach that enables effective few-shot anomaly detection using only normal samples by simulating anomalies and promoting interaction between textual and visual modalities. | The key contribution of this paper is proposing AnoPLe, a multi-modal prompt learning method for few-shot anomaly detection that does not require any prior knowledge or annotations of true anomalies. AnoPLe simulates anomalies and employs bidirectional coupling of learnable textual and visual prompts to enable deep interaction between the two modalities for effective anomaly detection with limited normal training data. It also introduces a lightweight decoder to enhance anomaly localization by learning local semantics from multi-view inputs during training. AnoPLe achieves strong few-shot anomaly detection performance comparable to state-of-the-art methods that rely on true anomaly annotations, with only around 1% gap in image-level AUROC on MVTec-AD and VisA datasets.
[QA 4] The paper provides a comprehensive analysis of vacuous reduct semantics in abstract argumentation frameworks, offering criteria for the inheritance of principle satisfaction and conducting a principled analysis for undisputed semantics, thereby expanding the understanding and applicability of these semantics in the context of weak argumentation. | Thank you for the detailed paper. Here is a one-sentence summary of the key contribution:

The paper provides a systematic overview and principled analysis of vacuous reduct semantics for abstract argumentation, obtained by combining different admissibility-based and conflict-free semantics, and characterizes criteria for principle satisfaction by these semantics.
